\paragraph{Per-benchmark contamination effects.}
The primary model assumes a global~$\gamma$ shared across all benchmarks. As an ablation, we consider a per-benchmark variant:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:per_benchmark_gamma}
    P(y_{ij} = 1 \mid \boldsymbol{\psi}_k) = c_j + (d_j - c_j)\,\sigma\!\bigl(a_j(\theta_i - b_j) + \gamma_k \cdot \tau_{ij}\bigr),
\end{equation}
where $\gamma_k$ is the contamination effect specific to benchmark~$k$, with independent priors $\gamma_k \sim \mathcal{N}(0, 1)$. This variant can capture heterogeneous contamination effects---for instance, knowledge-intensive benchmarks (MMLU) may exhibit larger contamination inflation than multi-step reasoning benchmarks (GSM8K)---at the cost of reduced statistical power per benchmark. We compare both specifications in \S\ref{sec:experiments}.

